# Design Judgment

Rules guide judgment; they do not replace it. If applying a rule makes the screen uglier, the application is wrong.

## Restraint

- Semantic red/green = system states only (error/success), not decoration
- Non-neutral colors on a screen: if >2, too many
- Prefer 1 accent + neutrals

## Don't Fix What Isn't Broken

If a proven pattern already looks good, don't change it only because a rule seems to conflict. Appearance is the judge.

## Practical Defaults

- Header/toolbar icons: tappable containers, same size (~40–44px)
- If content fills >80% of viewport, too dense — aim 60–70% content
- List items of the same function must match visually (except content)
- Hero of a screen may be centered for visual gravity

## When Rules Conflict

1. Build following rules → screenshot → “does it look premium?”
2. If no, adjust interpretation; keep the spirit
3. Better-looking version wins

## When to Break a Rule

Only if you can name the rule, explain why it fails the user here, keep it reversible, and expect most designers would agree.

## Maturity Ladder

Functional → Consistent → Polished → Delightful → Adaptive. Never skip ahead.
